C. Voiceover script 
1. OPENING (0:00–1:00)
A ride app can tell you your car arrives in four minutes. But for life-critical health deliveries, there is still no prediction at the moment an order is placed.
The stakes are treatment continuity: when HIV medicines and test kits arrive late, patients risk interrupted treatment. Logistics officers do sense delays through calls and emails with carriers and forwarders. But that signal is manual and inconsistent, and global logistics is hit by the unexpected: customs holds, cancelled flights, port congestion. Warnings get missed or come too late.
We are V12 Engines. We use 10,324 real USAID and PEPFAR shipments from 2006 to 2015. About 11.5 percent arrived late, and the delays are not random: shipments routed From RDC make up 78 percent of all late deliveries.
2. SOLUTION (1:00–1:40)
The moment an order is placed, two Snap ML models score it: one predicts the probability of being late, the other estimates how many days late. IBM Granite explains why, using only the model's top drivers, and recommends an action matched to the runway: how many days remain until the promised delivery. The dashboard ranks alerts by risk, and a logistics officer makes the final call.
3. WHY IBM Z + LIVE DEMO (1:40–2:10) (caption: "Replay of 2006–2015 shipments, not a live feed")
Here we replay historical shipments as if they were arriving live. Each alert shows probability, estimated days late, and a runway badge that turns redder as time runs out. The officer acknowledges, takes action, or dismisses, and every decision is logged. Everything runs on IBM LinuxONE: [X: only evidence Ethan verified, e.g. s390x, machine type, Podman containers; mention the NNPA accelerator only if confirmed enabled].
4. RESULTS (2:10–2:35)
